# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.Python
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Runtime state: written by `atlas up`, machine-specific, never committed.
launched.json
launched.json.tmp

# App state and per-chat workspaces (atlas.db, data.duckdb, blobs/, ...).
# Everything the user has uploaded or generated lives here.
data/
workspace/

# Strands' own snapshot tree (.agent/sessions/<chat>/...). Written relative to
# the process working directory, so it lands at the repo root when the backend
# is launched from there. This is conversation history, not source: committing
# it would bury every message and tool result in the diff. `tests/.agent` is
# covered by the same rule -- it was branch-leak evidence, and it is evidence
# only if something can still go wrong.
.agent/
**/.agent/

# Generated test corpus: `python -m agent.ingest.samples` rebuilds it byte for
# byte from agent/ingest/samples.py, so committing it would only create merge
# conflicts on a directory that is derived output.
samples/

# Model weights: ~6.5 GB of GGUF. Never in git. Individual files run to
# 2.6 GB, far past GitHub's 100 MB hard limit, and the git-lfs free tier
# (1 GB storage, 1 GB/month bandwidth) is exhausted by a single push. The
# directory is ignored outright rather than by extension so that a new model
# dropped in later is also excluded by default.
llm/

# Local configuration. settings.json holds absolute paths to this machine's
# GGUF and mmproj, so it is per-machine by nature. settings.example.json is
# the committed template; copy it and fill in the two paths.
settings.json
settings.local.json
# Per-test settings files written by the probe scripts in %TEMP%\atlas_spikes.
# Each one is a copy of settings.json with one key changed, so committing any of
# them would only risk a stale path ending up in a real config.
settings_*.json

# Local secrets / overrides
.env
.env.*

# Logs. samples/server.log is covered by samples/ above; this catches the
# launcher and backend writing into the working directory.
*.log

# Stray exports. The SQL guard blocks `COPY ... TO`, but the escape test in
# L4 proved DuckDB's read_only is not a boundary, so a stray artifact landing
# in the repo root is worth never committing by accident.
*.csv.bak
/atlas_escape*
/out.csv

# Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
